  "text": "Solution 2 is correct, with $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+4}{N}$.\n\nUnder the $L^2$-preserving rescaling $u_\\lambda = \\lambda^{N/2}u(\\lambda x)$, the kinetic term scales as $\\lambda^2$. The lower critical term $\\int(I_\\alpha*|u|^{\\bar p})|u|^{\\bar p}$, with $\\bar p = \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N}$, has exponent $N\\bar p - N - \\alpha = 0$, so it is invariant. The cross term $\\iint |u|^{\\bar p}(x)\\,|u|^q(y)/|x-y|^{N-\\alpha}$ scales as $\\lambda^{(Nq-N-\\alpha)/2}$, which I checked by change of variables. Balancing this against the kinetic exponent 2 gives $Nq - N - \\alpha = 4$, so $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+4}{N}$.\n\nSolution 2 also checks that this value lies in the admissible interval $\\left(\\frac{N+\\alpha}{N}, \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}\\right)$ exactly when $\\alpha > N-4$. That matches the problem's hypothesis $\\alpha > \\max\\{0, N-4\\}$, which is strong evidence for this threshold.\n\nSolutions 1 and 3 use the single-power mass-critical exponent $1 + \\frac{\\alpha+2}{N}$, which ignores that the lower critical term is itself scale-invariant under this rescaling. Their candidate $\\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$ is also always admissible (it needs only $\\alpha > -2$), so it gives no matching check against the hypothesis.\n\nI did not verify this against the source paper; the answer rests on the scaling argument and the range check.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
